+The overlap gains reflect a balance between different errors. A constraint
+can reduce missed parts of a structure while adding a smaller number of
+incorrect foreground voxels elsewhere. Across the adaptation studies,
+the selected objectives recover more foreground but also extend further
+into the surrounding background.
+
+MedSAM3 on MSD provides a concrete example. With all four constraints,
+the mean number of missed foreground voxels falls by about 313 per
+validation volume, while extra foreground increases by about 126 voxels.
+Anterior/posterior swaps also decrease. The reduction in misses is thus
+about two and a half times the increase in extra foreground. Expressed
+as percentages of their respective baseline counts, these changes are
+$-36.0\%$ and $+40.7\%$: the larger percentage increase starts from a
+smaller count and does not mean that it cancels the reduction in misses.
+These are study averages, not a guarantee for every volume. Dice gives
+false positives and false negatives symmetric roles within each class;
+the improvement reflects the resulting overlap, not a lower penalty
+assigned to the additional errors.
+
